 of the recommendation covered, \"R51-1\". Hmm, but the (A|B) constraint...\n\nAlternatively, maybe scheme refers to whether the certificate is issued under the MAA (Mutual Acceptance Arrangement) vs Basic. The prompt says scheme (A|B). In OIML-CS documents, \"OIML certificate scheme\" — certificates state \"issued under the Basic System\". Hmm.\n\nI'll go with scheme as the part designation of the recommendation: The certificate says \"R51-1, edition 2006\". So recommendation id \"R51\", edition 2006, and scheme... Actually, for R51, part 1 is \"Automatic catchweighing instruments - Part 1: Metrological and technical requirements - Tests\". Part 2 is \"Part 2: Test report format\". So the \"scheme\" could be \"1\" or \"R51-1\".\n\nGiven the schema says (A|B), maybe for some recommendations there are schemes A and B (like R137 gas meters have scheme A/B?). For R51, no scheme. I'll omit or set null... Actually let me reconsider: maybe this is the OIML-CS certificate type. OIML-CS issues \"OIML Certificates\" (basic) vs \"OIML MAA Certificates\". Not A/B.\n\nHmm, alternatively \"scheme\" might be like the letter in certificate numbers like \"R60/2000-DE1-xx.xx\" — no.\n\nI think safest: set certificate.scheme to null or omit. But wait — the prompt gives the example `recommendation: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]`. The recommendation scheme — for R51-1 there's no scheme letter. Hmm, but some recommendations like R137 have... Actually, R76 (NAWIs) doesn't have schemes. R60 load cells don't have A/B scheme.\n\nActually! I remember: OIML-CS basic documents describe \"Scheme A\" and \"Scheme B\" for certificates? Hmm, in OIML B 15? No...\n\nLet me just make a decision: I'll omit scheme at certificate level or set it to null, and for recommendation I'll not include a scheme either since R51 doesn't define one. Actually, maybe include \"scheme\": \"R51-1\" — no, that conflates.\n\nHmm, actually re-reading: