ontent. Since I can reason that R51 uses X(III), I'll normalize to \"X(III)\" and \"X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\". Hmm, but footnote 4 references \"categories XIIIH(x ≥ 2) and Y(b)\" — Y(b) is a real MID category for automatic catchweighers. So the cert covers categories X(III)H(x≥2) and Y(b)? And common spec says \"Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" → \"X(III); X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\".\n\nHmm, wait. Actually MID for automatic catchweighers (MI-6? no, automatic catchweighers aren't in MID... Actually MID Annex I-06? No: MID includes automatic catchweighing instruments in Annex I-06? Let me recall: MID Annexes: AI (water meters), AII (gas), AIII (electricity), AIV (heat), AV (measuring systems liquids), AVI (weighing instruments — NAWIs), AVII (taximeters), AVIII (material measures), AIX (dimensional), AX (exhaust gas). So NAWIs only — not automatic catchweighers. But the footnote says \"category Y according to MID, Annex I, chap. II No 5\" — hmm, that's odd. MID Annex I? Whatever — the cert references MID, so the cert covers categories X and Y in some form. R51-1:2006 does align with MID: classes X, XI, XII, and Y per MID Annex I. Yes! R51-1:2006 defines accuracy classes X, XI, XII (corresponding to MID classes X(1), X(2), X(5)...?) Hmm, no...\n\nActually now I remember better: MID Annex I chapter II covers... no wait. MID Annex I is \"Metrological requirements\"? The OIML R 51-1:2006 was harmonized with MID 2004/22/EC where NA WIs... no, automatic catchweighers ARE in MID! MID Annex I-06 is \"Automatic weighing instruments\" covering automatic catchweighing instruments (category X and Y)! Yes — MID 2004/22/EC Annex I-06: Automatic weighing instruments: 6.1 automatic catchweighing instruments. And classes: X, XI, XII (for automatic gravimetric?) Hmm. MID I-06 defines for automatic catchweighers the classes X, XI, XII and Y? No...\n\nOK here's the real story (I'm fairly confident): For automatic catchweighing instruments, the accuracy classes in R51/MID are X, XI, XII — where the class is written as X with a roman numeral indicating the interval class for static weighing: X(I), X(II), X(III). So \"XIII\" = \"X(III)\"! The classes are written without parentheses in some documents: \"Class XIII\" doesn't exist; it's X(III). MID Annex I-06 mentions \"category Y according to MID, Annex I, chapter II\"? Hmm, footnote says \"category Y according to MID, Annex I, chap. II No 5\" — MID Annex I chapter II is about... units? Whatever.\n\nSo: category \"X(III)\" and \"X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\" where H is a special/higher accuracy class handling multiple ranges (x is the number of partial ranges?). Good.\n\nDecision: I'll write accuracy_classes: [\"X(III)\", \"X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\"] — wait, but should I include Y(b)? Footnote 4 mentions Y(b) as another category for which n_i ≤ 1000 applies. The tables only list \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\". I'll include just the two main ones. Hmm, actually maybe I keep exactly as OCR: \"XIII(1)\". Ugh.\n\nLet me look at page 1 again: \"for accuracy class XIII(1)\". If it were X(III), the \"(1)\" would be \"(III)\"... OCR reading \"(III)\" as \"(1)\"? Possible if the III looks like 1? Hmm, \"III\" → \"1\"? Not typical; OCR usually gets roman numerals. But \"X(III)\" without spaces could be read \"XIII\" by dropping parens? That would give \"XIII\" not \"XIII(1)\".\n\nAlternatively the class is literally \"XIII(1)\"? Hmm, no such class.\n\nWait, maybe it's \"X(III)(1)\"? Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the OCR mangled \"X(III)\" → \"XIII\" by dropping the parentheses, and then where the source had \"X(III)(1)\"... no.\n\nActually here's another thought: R51-1:2006 accuracy classes per section: \"Class XIII\" no. Let me just recall the actual Bizerba CWM certificate R51/2006-DE1-07.05. I can't fetch it, but similar PTB R51 certs (e.g., Minebea Intec, Mettler) state \"accuracy class X(III) and Y(b)\" or \"X(III)\". The common spec \"Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" strongly suggests \"X(III); X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\". The \"H\" class with (x ≥ 2) is a 