Caption \"Tab. 4: WS10H\" after T_D.\nT_E: vmax 1.47/0.25/0.92/0.67/0.50; n ≤ 6000/5000, ≤ 6000(4); e ≥ 1 g; Max ≤ 15; Min ≥ 50 g.\nCaption \"Tab. 5: WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK\" after T_E.\nT_F: vmax 0.83/0.67/0.50; n ≤ 2•3750, ≤ 7500(4); e ≥ 0.1 g; Max ≤ 3750 g; Min ≥ 10 g.\nCaption \"Tab. 6: EM ...\" after T_F.\n\nHmm wait, but that mapping gives weird results: WS10CW/WS20CW with e ≥ 1 g fine; WS20VB e ≥ 2 g; WS6H e ≥ 5 g, Max 30 kg; WS10H e ≥ 10 g, Max 60 kg — hmm, but WS10H \"H\" might mean \"high capacity\"? WS10 = 10 kg? But T_D says Max ≤ 60 kg. WS6H suggests 6 kg. WS20VB suggests 20 kg but T_C says Max ≤ 30 kg. Hmm, the numbering may relate to different specs (belt width?).\n\nAlternatively captions ABOVE tables: Then Tab.1 (WS10CW/WS20CW) = T_B? No — caption \"Tab. 1\" appears after T_A and before T_B. In markdown reading order: table, caption, table, caption... If captions are above their tables, then Tab.1 labels T_B, Tab.2 labels T_C, Tab.3 labels T_D, Tab.4 labels T_E, Tab.5 labels T_F, Tab.6 labels nothing (next page? there is no more table — page 4 ends with the note). That would leave Tab. 6 dangling, which is wrong since Tab. 6 (EM ...) must have a table. So captions must be BELOW their tables: Tab.1→T_A, Tab.2→T_B, Tab.3→T_C, Tab.4→T_D, Tab.5→T_E, Tab.6→T_F. \n\nHmm wait, but the first table T_A appears right after \"### Specifications depending on the module used\" with no intro; and then \"Tab. 1\" caption below it. Yes, German standard: table caption below (\"Tab. 1: ...\"). So mapping:\n\n- WS10CW, WS20CW: T_A (e ≥ 1 g, Max ≤ 15 kg, Min ≥ 50 g, vmax ≤ 1.47 m/s, n ≤ 6000/5000 or ≤ 5000)\n- WS20VB: T_B (e ≥ 2 g, Max ≤ 15 kg, Min ≥ 200 g, vmax load-dependent)\n- WS6H: T_C (e ≥ 5 g, Max ≤ 30 kg, Min ≥ 1 kg, vmax ≤ 0.83 m/s)\n- WS10H: T_D (e ≥ 10 g, Max ≤ 60 kg, Min ≥ 3 kg, vmax load-dependent 0.67/1.00)\n- WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK: T_E (e ≥ 1 g, Max ≤ 15 kg, Min ≥ 50 g, vmax load-dependent 1.47/0.25/0.92/0.67/0.50)\n- EM ...: T_F (e ≥ 0.1 g, Max ≤ 3750 g, Min ≥ 10 g, vmax load-dependent 0.83/0.67/0.50)\n\nHmm, wait: WS20VB with Max ≤ 15 kg? Name says WS20... And WS6H Max ≤ 30? WS10H Max ≤ 60? That seems off (a \"6\" model with Max 30 kg?). But names might not correspond to Max directly (could be belt length or platform size). Actually hold on, maybe captions are above: Tab.3 (WS6H) = T_D? No wait, if above: Tab.1→T_B (WS10CW/WS20CW: e≥2g, Max 15, Min 200g), Tab.2→T_C (WS20VB: e≥5g, Max 30, Min 1kg), Tab.3→T_D (WS6H: e≥10g, Max 60, Min 3kg), Tab.4→T_E (WS10H: e≥1g, Max15, Min 50g), Tab.5→T_F (WS10VK..., e≥0.1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g), Tab.6→nothing. Tab.6 with no table is impossible. Also the very first table T_A would be an unlabeled intro table for \"modules\" generally? Hmm, \"Specifications depending on the module used\" followed by a table then Tab.1 caption...\n\nBut wait — actually with German DIN style, \"Tab. 1: ...\" captions are typically BELOW tables. And T_A before Tab.1 caption... but then what about the table right after the heading? It IS the table captioned Tab.1. OK so below-caption mapping stands.\n\nSanity check with model names: WS10VK1/VK2/VK5 with e ≥ 0.1 g? No wait, under below-caption mapping, WS10VK* get T_E: e ≥ 1 g, Max ≤ 15 kg. And EM... gets e ≥ 0.1 g, Max ≤ 3750 g (3.75 kg). Hmm, EM = electromagnetic force compensation → small capacity (3750 g = 3.75 kg) — that makes sense! EM series are force-compensation, small Max. ✓. And WS = strain gauge. WS6H: Max 30 kg? Hmm. WS10H Max 60 kg. WS20VB Max 15 kg? That's odd (20 > 15). With above-caption mapping: WS10CW/WS20CW Max 15, WS20VB Max 30, WS6H Max 60?? worse. Below-caption: WS6H Max 30, WS10H Max 60 — H = heavy? 6H with Max 30? Hmm, \"WS6H\" maybe belt length 6 dm? Whatever. The suffix H might mean \"heavy duty frame\". WS10CW Max 15 kg ✓ (10 → 15 kg plausible), WS20VB Max 15?? VB = ... hmm.\n\nActually hold on, let me reconsider: maybe the first table (T_A) belongs to the header \"Specifications depending on the module used\" as a general base spec, and Tab.1 caption below T_A is just placed under it... In DIN, caption below the table it describes. So T_A ↔ Tab.1. Fine.\n\nActually, wait. Let me recount the markdown order on page 3:\n\n```\n### Specifications depending on the module used\n\n<table> T_A: vmax ≤1.47; n ≤6000/5000 & ≤5000; e ≥1g; Max ≤15kg; Min ≥50g </table>\n\n**Tab. 1:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\n\n<table> T_B: vmax load-dep; n ≤2•3000 & ≤3000; e ≥2g; Max ≤15; Min ≥200g </table>\n\n**Tab. 2:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS20VB\n\n<table> T_C: vmax ≤0.83; n ≤2•3000 & ≤6000; e ≥5g; Max ≤30kg; Min ≥1kg </table>\n\n**Tab. 3:** Technical data of the weighing module of type WS6H\n\nPage 3 of 4\n---\nPage 4:\n<table> T_D: vmax 0.67(0<L<3)/1.00(3≤L≤60); n ≤2•3000 & ≤6000(3); e ≥10g; Max ≤60; Min ≥3kg </table>\n\n**Tab. 4:** Technical data of the weighing module of type WS10H\n\n<table> T_E: vmax 1.47/0.25/0.92/0.67/0.50; n ≤6000/5000 & ≤6000; e ≥1g; Max ≤15; Min ≥50g </table>\n\n**Tab. 5:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\n\n<table> T_F: vmax 0.83/0.67/0.50; n ≤2•3750 & ≤7500; e ≥0.1g; Max ≤3750g; Min ≥10g </table>\n\n**Tab. 6:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type EM ...\n```\n\nCaption-below mapping:\n- Tab.1 WS10CW & WS20CW: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax≤1.47, n≤6000/5000(≤5000)\n- Tab.2 WS20VB: e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g, vmax load-dep, n≤2•3000(≤3000)\n- Tab.3 WS6H: e≥5g, Max 30kg, Min 1kg, vmax≤0.83, n≤2•3000(≤6000)\n- Tab.4 WS10H: e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg, vmax load-dep, n≤2•3000(≤6000)\n- Tab.5 WS10VK1/2/5, WS20VK: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax load-dep (1.47/0.25/0.92/0.67/0.50), n≤6000/5000(≤6000)\n- Tab.6 EM...: e≥0.1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g, vmax load-dep (0.83/0.67/0.50), n≤2•3750(≤7500)\n\nCheck consistency: common spec says Max ≤ 60 kg overall, e ≥ 0.1 g overall, n ≤ 7500. Tab.6 (EM) has Max 3750 g and e ≥ 0.1 g and n ≤ 7500 — these define the extremes ✓. vmax ≤ 1.47 m/s overall — Tab.1 has ≤1.47 ✓ and Tab.5 has 1.47 in a band ✓. Min ≥ 10 g smallest ✓ (Tab.6). Great, the common specs are the envelope of all modules — consistent with below-caption mapping. ✓\n\nSo the model family: the certified type \"CWM ...\" checkweigher with weighing modules: WS10CW, WS20CW, WS20VB, WS6H, WS10H, WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, EM...(series).\n\nModel family models: I'll create model entries per module type:\n- WS10CW, WS20CW (share Tab.1 data)\n- WS20VB (Tab.2)\n- WS6H (Tab.3)\n- WS10H (Tab.4)\n- WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK (share Tab.5 data)\n- EM ... (series, Tab.6)\n\nFor model_level characteristics, attributes per module group. Since some modules share identical data, I can group values per model id (each model listed).\n\nNow characteristics:\n\ntype_level (common specifications):\n- mode_of_operation: \"Start-Stop operation or operation in motion\" — string value.\n- maximum_belt_speed: {max: 1.47} m/s — note \"≤ 1,47 m/s\" → value {min: null, max: 1.47}, unit \"m/s\". (1,47 → 1.47)\n- maximum_capacity: {max: 60} kg\n- verification_scale_interval: {min: 0.1} g (\"e ≥ 0.1 g\")\n- number_of_verification_scale_intervals: {max: 7500} — attribute `verification_scale_intervals_n`, value {min:null, max:7500}, unit null. Common spec: n ≤ 7500.\n- power_supply_voltage: 230 V, power_supply_type AC, power_supply_frequency {50,60} Hz.\n- category: \"X(III); X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\" — hmm as string. I'll put category: \"X(III); X(III)H(x ≥ 2)\" as type_level characteristic? It's already in accuracy_classes of recommendation and category of certified_type. I could include as type_level attribute \"category\". Maybe include for completeness.\n- number_of_ranges_intervals: 2 with footnote markers 2), 3). \"Number of ranges/intervals: 2^{2) 3)}\" — value 2, footnotes [\"2)\", \"3)\"].\n- ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals: \"e_{i+1}/e_i < 3\" with footnotes 1), 2). Value: formula/symbol — \"e_{i+1}/e_i < 3\"? As string \"e(i+1)/e(i) < 3\"? The rule says no LaTeX. I could write attribute `verification_scale_interval_ratio_e_next_over_e` and value string \"< 3\"? Hmm. The value is \"e_{i+1}/e_i < 3\". I'd record attribute `ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals` value \"e(i+1)/e(i) < 3\" as string, footnote_markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"]. Or value: {max: 3} (exclusive)? Simpler: string \"e_{i+1}/e_i < 3\" — but avoid underscores/LaTeX-ish. Use \"e(i+1)/e(i) < 3\". Good enough.\n\nAlso note \"Number of ranges/intervals: 2\" — with sup markers 2) 3). Value 2, dimensionless.\n\nconfig/module_level: This is where the six tables go. Options: matrix_tables[] with one entry per table, plus model_level characteristics. Given the load-dependent vmax entries are config-level (condition on load L), I should use config_level for vmax vs load, and model_level for e, Max, Min, n.\n\nLet me define model ids: I'll use the module type names as model ids:\n- \"WS10CW\", \"WS20CW\" — wait, the caption says \"weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\" — both share the same table. Should models[] have separate entries for each? Yes, one entry per variant: model_id \"WS10CW\", \"WS20CW\" with attributes from Tab.1. Similarly \"WS10VK1\", \"WS10VK2\", \"WS10VK5\", \"WS20VK\" share Tab.5. And \"EM ...\" series — model_id \"EM ...\"? The type_designations mention \"EM ...\" as a series. Hmm. I'll use model_id \"EM (series)\" or \"EM ...\". The certificate literally writes \"EM ...\". I'll use \"EM ...\" — hmm, maybe better \"EM series\". Let me keep as printed: \"EM ...\". Hmm, trailing \"...\" might look odd but it's faithful. Actually for cleanliness: model_id: \"EM ... (electromagnetic force compensation series)\"? Don't fabricate — description says \"with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM...)\". So \"EM...\" is the series. I'll use model_id \"EM...\". Eh, I'll use \"EM ...\".\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether models should be the full checkweigher configurations or just modules. The certified type is CWM checkweigher; the tables give \"technical data of the weighing modules\". So components[]: weighing modules as components with type_designations. And model_family could be the module variants. The schema says model_family include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant — here yes, multiple module variants.\n\nI'll structure:\n- certified_type.type_designations: [\"CWM\", \"WS10CW\", \"WS20CW\", \"WS20VB\", \"WS6H\", \"WS10H\", \"WS10VK1\", \"WS10VK2\", \"WS10VK5\", \"WS20VK\", \"EM ...\"] — hmm, CWM is the instrument type; modules are components. Maybe type_designations: [\"CWM ...\"] and components[]: weighing modules. Let me do:\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Checkweigher\"\n- type_designations: [\"CWM\"]\n- module_designation: null — the schema's module_designation examples are for load cells (\"Analog load cell\"). Here not applicable → \"Not applicable\"? For this cert, module concept (per R76) doesn't apply. I'll omit or set null. Set null.\n- description: \"Automatic electromechanical weighing instrument designed as automatic catchweighing instrument, checkweigher, weigh labeller or weigh-price labeller; equipped with external lever work (only weighing module series WS6H and WS10H) or without external lever work and with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM...) or with strain gauge load cells; performed as single or multi interval instrument or single or multiple range instrument.\"\n\ncomponents[]:\n- role: \"weighing module\", type_designations: all module types, alternatives null.\n\nOr individual components? Rule 6: components for complex instruments. I'll add one component \"weighing module\" listing all types. Maybe better: since data is per module group, I'll rely on matrix_tables + model_level for the data.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null (no explicit family name; \"CWM\" could be the family). Actually family_name could be \"CWM\". The type is \"CWM ...\"